Z||000024||JohnnyK||12-21-2000||01:57 PM||||Why would it fall on it's face at 120? Not geared for that? So, if these things can leave black marks till I let off the throttle, your saying they ARE faster than todays carS?||||reg||1
Z||000025||Fieroboy1||12-21-2000||06:05 PM||||well i've ridden in a 70 big block chevelle.  It was about as fast as my dad's 84 350 powered fiero with the 4.10 transaxle. As far as being ideal, a 350 fiero is not.  It's hard to make a v8 fiero dependable like a stock vehicle due to the nature of the beast.  v8 fieros are fun in a straight line and they are good for around town but i found them to be useless for anything but that purpose.  As far as the modern vs. newer vehicle.  Muscle cars are great for nostalgia, there's the nice quality of not having emissions testing on them.....etc.  However,  Today's technology is just so much better.  I have driven a 2001 ss camaro and let me tell you it would take one hairy monster big block in a muscle car to hang with the new SS.  My brother drives a 93 z28.  The advancements in braking, handling and dependability coupled with the silky smooth hp and fuel effeciency of today's engines are just sooooo superior.  I say since you like the mustang go with a late 80's early 90's 5.0.  It is the most heavily supplied vehicle in the aftermarket.  You can go as fast as you're money can take you.  ||||reg||1
Z||000026||gixxer||12-21-2000||06:46 PM||||Umm ahhh errr. Weren't we talking about going really fast on the cheap?<BR>I had the opportunity to ride (many times, it was my brother-in-laws)in a 1967 L88 Corvette. With skinny slicks it would do 12.2 in the 1/4 mile @ 125 MPH. This was back in 1972. I believe small block Fieros are generally in this ET area. I don't believe any stock LT1 and later, powered Camaros or Firebirds are. <BR>The Fiero is perfectly suited to the addition of a little more weight, and a lot more power. Fieroboy, I never said a V-8 Fiero would handle as well as a late model Camoro or Porsche on a twisty road. But it is my firm belief that it could be be made to handle GOOD ENOUGH to get the job done. My 1997 Honda Civic (with sway bars)handles better than my "88 Formula. But the Fiero goes down the twisty bits much faster anyway, even in the rain. <BR>Impeccable handling doesn't translate into speed, it just makes the car easier to drive.<BR>Besides, if the car handles perfectly, it's boring to drive, right? =)<BR>I'm finishing up my turbo installation right now. I can't wait to find out how the car handles on a rain slicked corner when the boost comes in!<BR>Either you're in charge, or the car is.<BR>It's all a matter of attitude, right?<P> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000027||JohnnyK||12-21-2000||07:19 PM||||This isn't about if a Fiero can be made to go fast. I alredy have a Fiero, and I want to try something in the "big hairy beast" department..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> So I guess late 80's is the way to go? Get the Holley kit and some nitrous? (This is all if I had cash to spend). What could you get the HP up to? 350, 400?||||reg||1
Z||000028||Black88GT||12-21-2000||09:18 PM||||I have yet to see a SBC Fiero run 12s.  I have been asking for time slips ever since I can remember.<P>I would get a late 80s early 90s 5.0.  IMHO they look a lot better then the F-Bodies of the same years.  Install a NOS kit, 150 shot, and away you go.  Might only last you a couple months though (the car that is).<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
